{"content":[{"c":["ELATH"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Edomite city (also spelled Eloth) at the head of the Gulf of Aqaba (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.2.8","r":"Deut.2.8","rawR":"Deut.2.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.2.8","value":"Deut.2.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dt 2:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.9.26","r":"1Kgs.9.26","rawR":"1Kgs.9.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.9.26","value":"1Kgs.9.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 9:26"},"), on the eastern border of the wilderness of Paran (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.14.6","r":"Gen.14.6","rawR":"Gen.14.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.14.6","value":"Gen.14.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 14:6"},", where it is alternately called El-paran). It probably owed its named (which means “grove of trees”) to the many palm trees in the area and may have been located in a grove of sacred trees. Elath was strategically located along a primary trade route running from southern Arabia and Egypt to Phoenicia, making it a valuable city to possess."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Elath was taken by Kedorlaomer from the Horites (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.14.5-6","r":"Gen.14.5-6","rawR":"Gen.14.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.14.5-6","value":"Gen.14.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 14:5-6"},"). Later it was regarded as the southern limit of the territory of Edom (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.2.8","r":"Deut.2.8","rawR":"Deut.2.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.2.8","value":"Deut.2.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dt 2:8"},"). David probably captured it when he conquered Edom (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.8.14","r":"2Sam.8.14","rawR":"2Sam.8.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.8.14","value":"2Sam.8.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 8:14"},"). During the reign of Joram, Jehoshaphat’s son, revolt restored it to the Edomites (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.8.20-22","r":"2Kgs.8.20-22","rawR":"2Kgs.8.20-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.8.20-22","value":"2Kgs.8.20-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 8:20-22"},"). A few years later it was recaptured and rebuilt by Judah’s King Uzziah (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.14.22","r":"2Kgs.14.22","rawR":"2Kgs.14.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.14.22","value":"2Kgs.14.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 14:22"},"). It remained under Judah’s rule until the time of Ahaz, when it was taken by Rezin of Syria and occupied by Syrians (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.16.6","r":"2Kgs.16.6","rawR":"2Kgs.16.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.16.6","value":"2Kgs.16.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 16:6"},"). From about 753 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"}," onward, it remained an Edomite city until it was abandoned sometime between the sixth and fourth centuries ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},". Then the Nabateans, who controlled the area, built a city a little farther east of the original site and renamed it Aila."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=ElParan_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"ElParan_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"ElParan_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"El-paran"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Elath","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Dt 2:8","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Deut.2.8","rawQueryValue":"Deut.2.8","sourceRefExact":"Deut.2.8"},{"anchorText":"1 Kgs 9:26","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=1Kgs.9.26","rawQueryValue":"1Kgs.9.26","sourceRefExact":"1Kgs.9.26"},{"anchorText":"Gn 14:6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.14.6","rawQueryValue":"Gen.14.6","sourceRefExact":"Gen.14.6"},{"anchorText":"Gn 14:5-6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.14.5-6","rawQueryValue":"Gen.14.5-6","sourceRefExact":"Gen.14.5-6"},{"anchorText":"Dt 2:8","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Deut.2.8","rawQueryValue":"Deut.2.8","sourceRefExact":"Deut.2.8"},{"anchorText":"2 Sm 8:14","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Sam.8.14","rawQueryValue":"2Sam.8.14","sourceRefExact":"2Sam.8.14"},{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 8:20-22","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.8.20-22","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.8.20-22","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.8.20-22"},{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 14:22","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.14.22","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.14.22","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.14.22"},{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 16:6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.16.6","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.16.6","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.16.6"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e9dbcb4ef31e44f5c1d0b77d15ca755ec3e3c04e6835119fa53bddcfa79e8a0a","sourceItemName":"Elath","sourceItemOrder":59,"sourceMember":"Articles/E.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Elath","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Elath","title":"Elath","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}